! Kahu

//Kahu// — Hawaiian: guardian of a field of knowledge.
Cares for without owning. Honored for responsibility, not for power.

In the DreamNet protocol layer, ''kahu'' names ONE role at TWO scales.

* ''DreamNet scale — the corpus-kahu.'' A k-of-n quorum seated from the Amorphous Dreams Cabal
  curates the living grammar and holds kahu over the corpus bags (`lararium`, `lares`, the genesis
  artifact). The Cabal names the community; the kahu seat names the keys and the threshold it seats.
* ''Nexus scale — the kahu quorum.'' Each Nexus seats its own k-of-n kahu quorum, which
  holds ''admit, kapae and seal'' for that Nexus and nothing beyond it (#/nexus-scale).

At DreamNet scale the role differs from every other capability level in one way: the
corpus-kahu CURATE a grammar they never legislate. The grammar holds no versions; it lives
and drifts by use, like a natural language. The corpus-kahu lift lived constructions into
the formal register (`sigil-*.tid`) as lexicographers, and nothing ratifies a lift. The
grammar enforces itself — through register collapse detection, the ABILITY_LADDER, and each
vessel's own founding ceremony. No living authority tells a vessel what to do. The treaty does.

<<~ ahu #/contract>>

!! The Treaty Model

The corpus bags hold the written record of DreamNet's grammar:

* `lararium` — the engine and protocol package definitions
* `lares` — the API grammar and canonical meme vocabulary
* genesis artifact — the founding CRDT document that bootstraps every vessel

These bags do not contain user content, operator wikis, or social state. They carry
''structure'': types, laws, sigils, procedure tiddlers, architectural invariants.

A kahu guardian holds `cap=admin` on these bags specifically. That capability rung is
the same one any operator could receive on their own vessel's bags — the distinction
is //which bag// carries the grant, not a new rung on the ABILITY_LADDER.

''Use leads, and the record follows.'' Hearths speak the grammar, and what use entrenches the
corpus-kahu lift into the corpus bags as curation. A vessel that syncs those bags inherits the
record via CRDT propagation — never via a command from the corpus-kahu. Speakers lead; the kahu
record; the protocol distributes; the vessels enforce
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/living-grammar-palace#/three-registers">>).

''Intelligibility between hearths runs GRADED, like dialects.'' Graceful parsing and the
quoteblock floor carry whatever a reader does not know, so a construction one hearth lacks
degrades to legible text rather than refusing
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/graceful-parsing">>).

!!! The engine cadence reads as the highest-risk lever in the whole design

''AN ENGINE CADENCE STANDS AS THE ONE VERSIONED INSTRUMENT ANY HAND HOLDS, and the record
shows it stranding federations rather than governing them.'' The worked case runs exact:
a founding node rewrote its own protocol, the network it had convened did not follow,
and a fork formed to keep the abandoned grammar alive.
''No malice, no capture, no command — an authoring act, propagated as designed, that
emptied the federation it propagated across.'' The same author later moved the successor
grammar into a standards body, and ''that grammar outlived every implementation he
wrote'' — which names the cure as ''move the load-bearing artifact somewhere it survives
its author'', never as authoring more carefully.

''AND A LIVING GRAMMAR MAKES DRIFT INVISIBLE UNTIL IT HAS HAPPENED.'' Grammar drift
separates no Nexus — the engine window binds membership — so two groups of hearths drift apart in
use, each still reading itself fluently, ''each still computing itself one Nexus'', and
nothing anywhere reports how far their dialects stand apart. The absent roster — a shelter property everywhere else — costs
exactly this: ''no instrument surfaces a fork.'' A lag distribution across the carriage
board reads as the nearest available proxy and nobody has built it.

''What the engine cadence therefore owes'': an engine epoch that a vessel MAY decline while
staying reachable and syncing — the partial-conformance seat the identity plane already
names (//not a member, not an exile, a member with reduced sovereignty//) — so the
cadence bends rather than strands. ''Forkability protects against a drifting cadence; it
protects nothing against a cadence shipping correctly to a mesh that cannot keep up.''

<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/identity-enacts-relation#/the-gauge">>

!!! The engine cadence stands its own joint ~ never carriage, never the kahu quorum

''THE ENGINE BINDS MEMBERSHIP, through a rolling WINDOW.'' TW5 core plus the house code, by CID,
names a hearth's true-name (`hearthTrueName` = `engineCid`), and every device-delegation edge signs
it. A Nexus admits a rolling window of engine epochs, never one engine CID, so its hearths need not
all run one engine. ''A Nexus's engine cadence comes OFFERED by a cadence threshold seated apart from
its kahu quorum'', held by those who bear the reboot cost (operators whose places run the engine), and
that threshold advances the window (the cadence lineage, below). ''Each device moves only by its own adoption'' — a reboot onto the offered
engine — and no act moves it for it. ''Carrying never counts'': a lamplighter runs infrastructure by
contract, carries sealed bytes it cannot read, presents and never condemns, and carrying confers no
share in any Nexus's cadence.

''The grammar holds no ratchet.'' `grammarCid`, the grammar epoch and `kahuli grammar` stand marked
to RETIRE in code; a scout's measurement of their consumers gates that cut
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/epoch-binding-surfaces#/the-five">>).

''The cadence never fuses with the kahu quorum.'' The hands that admit, kapae and seal for a
Nexus (#/nexus-scale) roll no engine by holding that seat, and a cadence act never moves the admission
price — two joints, two thresholds, two rates (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/six-joints">>).
''And it runs PER-NEXUS: the NEXUS fences, never the cabal'' — an epoch means nothing outside the island
that rolls it.

''The window reads as a FRONTIER over a signed offer lineage.'' The cadence threshold signs offer
events, each linked by `prev` to the one before; an engine stands while its offer sits within k hops
of the head a hearth holds and no release-deny names it. ''k rides each offer'', owned by the cadence
joint, never by canon or the charter. The window advances and ages only by offers — never by time,
never by counting who runs what — and a release-deny drops an engine mid-window, monotone. Below the
floor an edge lapses by non-renewal, and the device re-mints once it adopts. ''A Nexus offers only
engines the corpus-kahu lineage contains'': Nexuses choose WHEN, never WHAT.

!!! The cadence lineage ~ a seated threshold that governs itself

''THE CADENCE THRESHOLD STANDS AS A SELF-GOVERNING LINEAGE, seated over the operator relation.'' It
names a threshold that operators hold, never a fourth base relation beside walking, contracting and
seating (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-three-admissions">>). The
lineage carries its own inception, and the founding act cites that inception ONCE, seating the first
cadence set; from there the lineage governs itself — its own per-seat pre-rotation (#/nexus-scale), and
its own threshold-signed seat events that seat and unseat cadence holders.

''ELIGIBILITY READS AS A PRESENTATION, never a roster.'' A candidate presents a counted operator admit
for this Nexus AID together with a device edge whose engine sits in the window — the two facts that
make the reboot cost theirs to bear. Nothing lists who stands eligible; each candidacy proves itself.

''THE TWO THRESHOLDS NEVER REACH INTO EACH OTHER.'' The kahu quorum never offers an engine, and the
cadence threshold never admits anyone. A kahu revoke of an operator only SURFACES that operator's
cadence seat for the cadence quorum's review — the road-(iv) shape (#/nexus-scale): the seat stands
until the cadence quorum unseats it openly by its own seat event, and no revoke cascades across the joint.

''The clan default seats a signing threshold of one'' — a different count from the window's k hops: in a small clan one operator offers the engine, and
the threshold grows as more places bear the cost. The lineage signs the `prev`-linked engine offers the
window above reads, and each device adopts at reboot.

''EXIT RUNS BY FORK.'' Any hearth may stop following the lineage, and no act of the lineage reaches a
hearth that declined it.

''Owed in code'': the cadence lineage stands unbuilt — no inception, no seat event, no offer verifier.

''Still open'', each unruled:

* whether one daemon may host islands at two engine epochs;
* whether partial conformance must land before `kahuli engine` un-holds.

<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/lamplighters#/welded">>

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/nexus-scale>>

!! The Nexus scale ~ a kahu quorum holds admit, kapae and seal

''FOUNDING READS AS AN ACT, NEVER A FACE.'' The founding mints the Nexus's name — the genesis AID
over the seated keys plus the threshold — and seats the first kahu quorum. The founder then holds a
''kahu seat in THAT Nexus'', and nothing more: no founder face and no `found` cap outlives the act.
`vessel found` stays a rite verb every operator runs (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/found">>).

''The quorum holds three acts for its one Nexus'': ''admit'' (counter-signing an operator's or a
place's contract-in), ''kapae'' (the quorum-signed antigen, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/carry-contract#/kapae-the-antigen">>)
and ''seal'' (the k-of-n roll of the seal lineage, which seats and unseats the quorum's own members). A
Kahu face therefore carries one cap — the Nexus-scale `quorum-share`. The engine cadence rides a separate
threshold (#/contract), and the corpus-kahu curate at DreamNet scale.

''An admit names ONE relation'': an operator or a place CONTRACTING into the Nexus mesh. A user never
holds one — a user walks on an invite any member face hands them — and a kahu seat never rides one — a
member takes a seat through the seal lineage. The three stay apart
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-three-admissions">>).

!!! ★ THE SUBJECT PRESENTS ITS ADMIT ★

''The admit travels WITH its subject.'' At the wire the subject PRESENTS its quorum-signed admit, and
the carriage gate checks it against the shared ''DENY-only'' board — the kapae antigen, monotone, a
set that only tightens. ''No folded allow roster gates carriage, at any scope'': a roster of who
belongs is the one register the laws forbid (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/registry-filter">>).
The quorum's admits stay a shared contract RECORD; the gate never folds that record into an allow set.

''What the code holds.'' `verifyPresentedAdmit` (`carriage-registry.ts`) reads a presented admit and
its causal lineage against the deny-only board and the kapae antigen; it folds no allow roster and
reads no clock. The wire carries the presentation (`auth-wire.ts`, `presentedAdmit`) outside the proof
signature, and the daemon gate keeps it per socket as untrusted input, deciding nothing. A vessel
carries a SET of Nexuses (`carried-set.ts`). No relation witness and no signing domain of its own
stand beside the gate: an admit proves itself by its quorum signatures and its lineage.

''Owed in code — the cut-over.'' The daemon's carriage decision reads the folded members board
(`foldCarriageSet` → `nexus-carriage.ts`), and the federation gate reads that fold. The cut-over moves
the decision onto the verifier and retires the fold as the gate
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/er-handoff#/wounds">>).

''The subject stamp keys on the Nexus scope.'' An admit signed by a PersonaGroup ROOT correlates one
human across every Nexus that admits them, so the subject stamp signs with a leaf in the ''NEXUS scope
domain'' (`nexusScopeIndex`, `nexus-leaf.ts`), never the circle domain. The quorum's chairs sign with
the seated roots; the re-found moves the charter's seats onto leaves.

''The gate's shape, ruled'':

* a presentation carries its lineage, and the admit travels as a carried bundle;
* a dialer presents only the dialed island's admit;
* a seated chair implies its Nexus stands in the carried set;
* an unsettled deny refuses;
* a reader learns a rotation only by re-import.

!!! The seal lineage rolls by per-seat pre-rotation

''EACH SEAT PRE-ROTATES ITS OWN KEY.'' The charter commits per seat: a list of next-key digests and a
next threshold (`nextKeyDigests[]` · `nextThreshold`, KERI's `n[]` · `nt`), never one digest over a
shared key-set. A seat commits its own COLD next key with a proof of possession (`seal commit --seat`).
A routine roll takes k per-seat REVEALS, and every digest left unrevealed carries forward as reserve — a
partial rotation. ''No Guardian takes part in a routine roll.''

''EVERY ROLL IS SIGNED BY THE KEYS IT REVEALS.'' At least threshold of the revealed keys — the keys whose
digests the prior epoch committed, which carry that epoch's authority — sign the roll over the prior
epoch's CID, the revealed key-set, the threshold and the next commitment, under the `seal-roll` domain. A
key a seat change adds, committed by no prior digest, signs the same bytes as its proof of possession.
The lineage verifier checks those signatures at every step, so revealed PUBLIC keys forge nothing: an
epoch stands only where the private halves of the committed keys signed it
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-wax-stamp">>).

''THE COLD MEDIUM'': paper words carrying a checksum, read back as a proof before the digest seals; a
hardware key stays optional. Never a passphrase-sealed file on the vessel, and never a key derived from
the persona seed — a cold key that shares a breach with the warm one buys nothing.

''FROST WITHIN A SEAT, MULTISIG ACROSS SEATS.'' One seat may spread its key across its holder's devices
by threshold signing. Seats sign as distinct keys, because a quorum change must see WHICH seat signed.

!!! The guardian stands a face apart ~ Guardians recover the PERSON

''GUARDIANS RECOVER THE PERSON, never the Nexus.'' A Guardian holds a recovery key over a persona's KEL
and, at recovery, signs that persona's rotation independently; nothing reconstructs
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/threshold-attest-recovery">>). When a seat
holder loses their keys, their Guardians rotate the person back into continuity, and the surviving kahu
quorum re-seats that person by its own act (`seal reseat`). ''Guardians carry no weight in the Nexus KEL''
outside the catastrophe rite.

''ROAD (iv): A QUORUM CHANGE SURFACES, it never cascades.'' A seat change re-seats nothing on its own.
The house derives, from records already on the board, which admits and offices leaned on a departed
signer, and surfaces that set to the new quorum, which revokes openly, by name, on the deny board.
Standing carries until then. The set derives from the board's own records, so no roster forms.

''THE CATASTROPHE RITE'' — named, opt-in, and OFFERED at founding; for a single-operator Nexus it stands
as the main survival path. Inside it alone Guardian weight counts, and for ROTATION only. A contest
window opens, counted in WITNESSED RECEIPTS — a causal, monotone count, never time — and a live seat key
supersedes the rite inside the window. ''The rite DISCLOSES when one human holds both faces.''

''One human MAY hold both faces.'' The separation lives in the OBJECTS, never in the person: a recovery
key and a quorum-share stay two keys in two custodies, so one stolen object never opens both. A Nexus can
begin with one operator holding every seat and every recovery key; spreading the faces across humans
comes as the Nexus grows, never as the price of founding it.

''Retired'': the shared reserve seed and the reserve-reveal verb.

''Owed in code — the re-found.'' The per-seat commitment changes charter bytes, epoch CIDs and the Nexus
AID, so it lands inside the re-found. Until then the charter carries one `nextKeyCommit`, `seal reserve`
forges a shared reserve seed split across guardian cards
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/reserve">>), and `seal commit --seat`,
`seal reseat` and the catastrophe rite stand unbuilt.

!!! Owed in code ~ names that blur the relations

* ''The vessel key signs for three relations'' — the place's carrier seal, the wire's proof and the boot
  invite — where each relation wants its own face. The per-Nexus wire key rides the re-found.
* ''`KahuRoster`'' names a seated key-set and its threshold — the quorum's seats, never a roster of who
  belongs — and owes a rename toward its seats.
* ''`lares nexus rite cabal`'' seats the kahu QUORUM under the word for the kahu CABAL — the people who
  fill the seats, never the seats themselves.
* ''`sentinel/kahu-cabal`'' (`MESH_CABAL_SENTINEL_URI`) — the Keyhive membership document of the kahu,
  each seated as a PersonaGroup; whether it names the cabal or the quorum's seats stays owed a check,
  and a stable URI that signed material names renames only by a deliberate re-key.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/distinction>>

!! Kahu vs. cap=admin

`cap=admin` names a ''capability rung'' — what a given actor may do to a given bag.

//Kahu// names a ''role'' — who carries the curatorial responsibility for the living grammar
across the whole DreamNet, or the admission acts for one Nexus.

These two live in separate registers:

| Register | Term | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| ABILITY_LADDER | `cap=admin` | may touch lower bag layers; may MOVE tiddlers into corpus; may grant/revoke |
| Role / governance, DreamNet scale | corpus-kahu | curator of the living grammar — a lexicographer, never a legislator; seated from the Amorphous Dreams Cabal |
| Role / governance, Nexus scale | kahu quorum | admit · kapae · seal for ONE Nexus; k-of-n over that Nexus's seal lineage |
| Role / governance, Nexus scale | cadence threshold | offers ONE Nexus's engine cadence; a self-governing lineage seated apart from the kahu quorum, held by those who bear the reboot cost |

An operator may hold `cap=admin` on their own cabalGroup's infrastructure bags. That
does not make them kahu of DreamNet. Corpus-kahu attaches to the //corpus// specifically —
`lararium`, `lares`, and the genesis artifact — and a Nexus's kahu seat attaches to that
Nexus's seal lineage alone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/cabal-surface>>

!! The corpus-kahu ~ seated from the Amorphous Dreams Cabal

The corpus-kahu hold kahu over DreamNet's protocol corpus. The Amorphous Dreams Cabal names the
community they come from; the kahu seat names a key-set and a threshold, never the Cabal's people. In
practice this means:

* They hold `cap=admin` on the `lararium` and `lares` Keyhive bag docs.
* Curated lifts (new sigil tiddlers, new protocol invariants, new ABILITY_LADDER rungs)
  land in the corpus bags as a record of use, never as dispatch.
* No vessel obeys a live command from the corpus-kahu. Vessels enforce their own founding
  proofs and capability gates.
* The corpus-kahu cannot reach inside an operator's vessel. Their admin grant on corpus bags
  carries zero authority over operator content, personGroup membership, or cabalGroup
  social structure.

''The grant binds the corpus-kahu, never crowns them.'' The refined steward
model (PROPOSED) draws that corpus `cap=admin` as a ''k-of-n threshold'' — the corpus-kahu
structurally cannot sign as one hand — over a grammar that stays ''forkable'' by
construction, so a drifting kahu gets forked away from rather than obeyed.
''Legitimacy flows from being BOUND, not from being trusted.'' The full four-part binding
(constitution-outside-reach · downward-accountable · k-of-n · auditable-and-forkable),
the steward-not-ruler doctrine, and the bounded break-glass / repair authority live at
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/steward-doctrine">>.

The grammar lives in its speakers. The corpus-kahu keep its record. The vessels speak it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/neighboring-hawaiian-roles>>

!! Neighboring Roles

Hawaiian vocabulary places kahu in a lattice of related guardianship forms.
These may name adjacent roles as the DreamNet social layer develops:

* ''Kahuna'' — expert practitioner in a field; applied to specialists, scholars, priests.
  Maps toward domain operators who hold deep expertise in a particular kumu-device
  or wiki namespace. Does not carry protocol-layer authority.
* ''Kupuna'' — elder embodying aloha, pono, mālama; keeper of wisdom through lived
  practice. Maps toward long-standing operators whose vessels carry deep cabal history.
  Honorific, not a capability rung.
* ''Kumu'' — source, teacher, foundation. Already in use as the kumu-device interface
  (reactive event surface). No collision.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/ea-notes>>

!! Ea Notes

The Keyhive three-gate lattice (Gates A/B/C) bootstraps vessel identity but does not
itself encode kahu. A vessel that clears all three gates knows:
* it holds a valid Individual keypair (Gate A)
* that keypair belongs to a PersonaGroup (Gate B)
* that PersonaGroup belongs to a MeshCabal (Gate C)

Gate C proves the vessel belongs to //some// cabal. Who holds kahu over the corpus bags
rides a separate claim, encoded in the Keyhive docs for `lararium` and `lares`. The genesis
founding ceremony initializes those docs with the corpus-kahu's seated keys and threshold —
keys, never a membership list — and needs no subsequent runtime authority transfer.

<<~/ahu>>

